1. XML declaration
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

This simply says:

This is an XML file
XML version = 1.0
Character encoding = UTF-8

You don't need to worry much about this part.

2. <web-app>
<web-app
    xmlns="https://jakarta.ee/xml/ns/jakartaee"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="https://jakarta.ee/xml/ns/jakartaee
    https://jakarta.ee/xml/ns/jakartaee/web-app_6_0.xsd"
    version="6.0">

This is the root element of web.xml.

Everything related to your web application goes inside:

<web-app>
    ...
</web-app>

You can think of it like:

web.xml
   |
   └── <web-app>
          |
          ├── servlet configuration
          ├── servlet mapping
          ├── welcome pages
          └── other configurations
What is version="6.0"?

It tells Tomcat that this configuration follows the Servlet 6.0 specification.

Since you're using Tomcat 11, Jakarta Servlet 6.0 is appropriate.

3. <servlet>

Now we have:

<servlet>
    <servlet-name>second</servlet-name>
    <servlet-class>SecondServlet</servlet-class>
</servlet>

This tells Tomcat:

"There is a servlet in my application."

4. <servlet-name>
<servlet-name>second</servlet-name>

This is simply a name that we give to the servlet inside the XML configuration.

We decided to call it:

second

It does not have to be the Java class name.

For example, this is also valid:

<servlet-name>myFirstServlet</servlet-name>

even if the Java class is:

SecondServlet

Think of it as an internal name/ID.

5. <servlet-class>
<servlet-class>SecondServlet</servlet-class>

This is very important.

It tells Tomcat:

"The servlet named second is implemented by this Java class."

So:

servlet-name
      ↓
   second
      ↓
servlet-class
      ↓
SecondServlet

If your Java class has a package:

package servlets;

public class SecondServlet extends GenericServlet {

}

then you write:

<servlet-class>servlets.SecondServlet</servlet-class>

Because the fully qualified class name is:

servlets.SecondServlet
6. Now <servlet-mapping>

This is where the URL comes into the picture.

<servlet-mapping>
    <servlet-name>second</servlet-name>
    <url-pattern>/second</url-pattern>
</servlet-mapping>

This tells Tomcat:

"When the user requests /second, execute the servlet named second."